# DHCP Configuration on a Wireless Router — Cisco Packet Tracer

A simulated network project configuring DHCP on a wireless router so connected devices automatically receive IP addresses, rather than requiring manual configuration.

## What's in this project

- `topology-screenshot.png` — the network topology (wireless router + connected devices)
- `dhcp-result-screenshot.png` — a connected device's IP configuration, showing an IP address automatically assigned by DHCP
- `dhcp-wireless-router.pkt` — the Packet Tracer project file

## What I built

- Configured a wireless router to act as a DHCP server, defining an IP address pool for client devices
- Connected multiple devices (PCs/laptops) to the wireless network
- Verified DHCP was working by checking a connected device's IP configuration and confirming it received an address automatically from the router's pool, with no manual IP setup required

## Why this matters

DHCP is one of the most common services a sysadmin manages — understanding how automatic IP assignment works (and how to verify it's functioning correctly) is directly relevant to real network administration and troubleshooting connectivity issues.

